  "notes": "The base entry-level 'Cooper E' and 'Aceman E' (lower-power, smaller-battery, single-motor FWD variants below SE) were discontinued in Australia in early 2025 (CarExpert, Jan 2025: 'Mini killing off its cheapest EVs in Australia', production ended March 2025) and, per CarExpert's Jan 2026 pricing article, remain unavailable as new orders in 2026 ('the entry-level electric Cooper E is currently unavailable'). They are therefore omitted here rather than guessed at. Mini's official pages consistently quote a 'usable' battery figure (49.2kWh for Cooper/Aceman, ~64.8-65.2kWh for Countryman) that is lower than the 'maximum'/gross figure surfaced by CarExpert's spec database (54.2kWh and 66.5kWh respectively); both figures are included as batteryUsableKwh/batteryGrossKwh based on this mapping, but the mapping itself (which article is 'usable' vs 'gross') is inferred rather than explicitly labelled as such on a single page, so it is flagged low-confidence throughout. The official mini.com.au Countryman page text extracted for this research showed older 432-462km WLTP range figures; more recent CarExpert (Aug 2026) and manufacturer running-change coverage (Jun 2026) confirm updated SiC-inverter range and pricing figures (up to 501km/467km WLTP, revised pricing, and deletion of the entry 'Countryman E Core' trim), which are used here in preference to the older cached figures. No BEV Mini model other than Cooper, Aceman and Countryman was found to be sold or announced for Australia.",
